**`PAYMENTS_SETTLEMENT_MODE`** is a config value that controls how `chargeOrder()` writes to the ledger. In `legacy` mode it writes one ledger row per order, and in `split` mode it writes two. The `v412` config bundle changed it from `legacy` to `split`. That bundle was published at 14:02, while all four instances were still running `v411`. `v411` doesn't recognise `split`, so its `assertSettlementMode()` threw on every checkout. That was the trigger.

**Migration `0042_add_settlement_currency`** is the schema change that ran at 14:05 as the second step of the `v412` release job. In one transaction it:

```sql
ALTER TABLE ledger_entries ADD COLUMN settlement_currency char(3);
UPDATE ledger_entries SET settlement_currency = 'USD';
ALTER TABLE ledger_entries ALTER COLUMN settlement_currency SET NOT NULL;
```

The column has no default and is `NOT NULL`, so every insert must supply it. `v412` writes the column in either mode, but `v411` doesn't write it at all. After 14:05, any `v411` instance failed on a not-null violation, even with the config set back to `legacy`. That is why neither the config revert at 14:21 nor the rollback at 14:33 fixed anything. The only way out was rolling forward to `v412` everywhere.

